<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>Graphs of Linear Equations</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m01903</md:content-id>
    <md:title>Graphs of Linear Equations</md:title>
    <md:uuid>97594100-8099-4879-912b-227b1f0d5c94</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
    <section class="numbered-exercises" id="sect-00001"><title>Warm Up</title>
      <para id="para-00001">Here is a graph of the equation <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>y</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>8</m:mn><m:mo>–</m:mo><m:mn>2</m:mn><m:mi>x</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>.</para>
      <para id="para-00002">
          <media alt="The graph of a line on a coordinate grid. The x-axis has a scale of 1 and extends from negative 2 to 5 while the y-axis has a scale of 2 and extends from negative 2 to 12.">
            <image mime-type="image/jpeg" src="../../media/Algebra-1_08_12_02_01.jpg" width="300"/>
          </media>
        
    </para>
      <!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
      <exercise id="exer-00001">
        <problem id="prob-00001">
          <para id="para-00003">Where do you see the 8 from the equation in the graph?</para>
          
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00001">
          <para id="para-00004">Compare your answer: </para>
          <para id="para-00005">The 8 in <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>y</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>8</m:mn><m:mo>–</m:mo><m:mn>2</m:mn><m:mi>x</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math> means the graph intersects the <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>y</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>-axis at 8. The <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>y</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>-intercept is (0,8).</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction End -->
      <!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
      <exercise id="exer-00002">
        <problem id="prob-00002">
          <para id="para-00006">Where do you see the –2 from the equation in the graph?</para>
          
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00002">
          <para id="para-00007">Compare your answer: </para>
          <para id="para-00008">The –2 is the slope of the line. You can see it, for example, by looking at <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mn>0</m:mn><m:mo>,</m:mo><m:mn>8</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo></m:mrow></m:math> and <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mn>1</m:mn><m:mo>,</m:mo><m:mn>6</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo></m:mrow></m:math>. When <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>x</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math> increases by 1, <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>y</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math> decreases by 2.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction End -->

        <exercise id="_2ce36a44-7afd-4ec4-b992-74d1eaaf0c8c" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem id="prob-00003">
            
            <para id="para-00009">What is the value of <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>x</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math> for the <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>x</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>-intercept of the graph? How does this relate to the equation?</para>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00003">
            <para id="para-00010">The <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>x</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>-intercept is <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mn>4</m:mn><m:mo>,</m:mo><m:mn>0</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo></m:mrow></m:math>. The 4 is the solution of <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mn>8</m:mn><m:mo>–</m:mo><m:mn>2</m:mn><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>0</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math>.</para>
           
          </solution>
        </exercise>

    </section>
  </content></document>